293,"1354":1098,"1355":975,"1356":1044,"1357":10},"orderedDocs":[[1304,"**A body is one argument of `field: value` pairs, separated by commas.** Put the whole body in\nsingle quotes. Put a value in double quotes if it contains a comma, looks like a number, is\n`true`, `false` or `null`, or starts with `[` or `{`. Unquoted, those are sent as something other\nthan text. For example `'title: \"Filed, then edited\"'`, `'title: \"123\"'` or `'title: \"true\"'`.\nA word after the body is refused rather than added to it."],[1305,"**A failed call still prints to the same stream a successful one would, and exits non-zero.**\nstlabs prints the response the same way on success or failure. `--query` and `--output` apply\nto a problem body as they apply to a real one, so a selected field prints `null` on a 404, not\nnothing. Check the exit code, as in `ID=$(stlabs ...) || exit`, not whether output came back\nempty."],[1339,"**Create and update.** Fields go in one argument of `name: value` pairs."],[1303,"**Every command that changes something is sent with an idempotency key.** stlabs generates a\nnew one each run, so running a command twice makes two changes. To make a retry safe, choose the\nkey yourself with `--idempotency-key <key>`. The same key with the same body returns the original\nresponse instead of a second ticket. It has the same `id` and the same status, and the header\n`Idempotent-Replayed` is `\"true\"`. The same key with a different body is refused with\n`IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_MISMATCH`. When a change fails in a way\nthat is safe to retry, stlabs prints the command to retry it with, key included, unless the\ncommand ran with `--query` or `--output` (the hint names the key alone then)."],[1337,"**List and read.** On a terminal a list is a table; everything else is JSON."],[1340,"**Script it.** Piped or redirected output is JSON, one record per command, so pipe it into jq or capture it. A command that fails exits with a code that says why."],[1307,"`--query` selects part of the response with a JMESPath expression, starting from the whole\nresponse (`status`, `headers` and `body`): `--query body.id` prints the id alone, which is how\nthe examples above capture a new ticket's id into a variable."],[1265,"`login` signs you in and `org set` picks the organization every call acts on. `status` shows the host, whether you are\nsigned in, and which organization is set. `logout` removes the stored token. See the two\nsections below for each."],[1266,"`login` stores the token in the OS keychain. On Linux that is the Secret Service when one is\navailable; on a headless machine set `STLABS_TOKEN` instead. It reads the token from stdin and\nrefuses one given as an argument, which would end up in your shell history:"],[1300,"`stlabs --help`, `stlabs tickets --help` and `stlabs tickets create --help` show the commands, the\narguments in order, the body fields and an example. Any other word is refused with\n`stlabs: unknown command`."],[1357,"`stlabs --version` prints the build's version and its API version."],[1267,"`STLABS_TOKEN` in the environment takes precedence over the keychain; use it in CI and scripts.\nWith neither, a command stops with `stlabs: not signed in` and sends nothing."],[1333,"<Callout type=\"info\" title=\"Access\">Access to the STLabs API is not open yet. This release cannot sign in or call the API. To request access, email support@stlabs.com.</Callout>"],[1336,"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"macOS\">macOS may block the first run of an unsigned app. If it does, open System Settings, Privacy & Security, and choose Open Anyway.</Callout>"],[1345,"<Card icon=\"BookOpen\" title=\"API reference\" href=\"/docs/api/\">Each operation with its parameters, request and response fields and error codes.</Card>"],[1346,"<Card icon=\"Package\" title=\"Releases\" href=\"https://github.com/Standard-Template-Labs/cli/releases\" external>every build, with checksums and notices.</Card>"],[1344,"<Card icon=\"Terminal\" title=\"Command line guide